WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.260
A GPU runs threads in thread blocks.

2
00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:06.410
A block runs on one streaming multiprocessor

3
00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:09.210
and its threads come in warps of thirty-two.

4
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.691
Many workloads need a great number

5
00:00:11.753 --> 00:00:13.320
of small matrix products.

6
00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:14.925
KAMI, from SC 2025

7
00:00:14.991 --> 00:00:17.368
brings communication-avoiding matrix

8
00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:19.745
multiplication into a single block

9
00:00:19.811 --> 00:00:21.660
the warps exchange as little

10
00:00:21.726 --> 00:00:22.848
data as possible

11
00:00:22.914 --> 00:00:25.226
and compute on the double-precision

12
00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:26.150
tensor cores.

13
00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:28.829
Each block computes C equals

14
00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:31.677
A times B transposed, plus half of C

15
00:00:31.677 --> 00:00:33.850
with eight bytes per number.

16
00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.800
Pause and predict.

17
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:38.875
The smallest public shape does twelve thousand

18
00:00:38.934 --> 00:00:41.067
two hundred eighty-eight operations

19
00:00:41.126 --> 00:00:42.666
and moves ten thousand two

20
00:00:42.725 --> 00:00:43.910
hundred forty bytes.

21
00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:47.327
An H100 needs about twenty operations per

22
00:00:47.402 --> 00:00:50.020
byte to keep its tensor cores busy.

23
00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:51.890
What limits this block?

24
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.270
Memory.

25
00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:58.510
The block does only one point

26
00:00:58.576 --> 00:01:00.140
two operations per byte.

27
00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:02.627
Times the bandwidth of three point

28
00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:04.576
three five terabytes a second

29
00:01:04.576 --> 00:01:07.090
that bounds it at about four teraflops

30
00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:09.290
far below the peak of sixty-seven.

31
00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:12.018
All four public shapes do between one

32
00:01:12.082 --> 00:01:13.946
and five operations per byte

33
00:01:14.011 --> 00:01:15.617
so the kernel's first job

34
00:01:15.682 --> 00:01:17.160
is to keep memory busy.

35
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:18.712
Inside a block

36
00:01:18.777 --> 00:01:20.981
the tensor cores work on fragments

37
00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:22.860
of sixteen by eight outputs.

38
00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:26.010
Say the warps form a grid over C.

39
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.669
Each warp reads its own strip of

40
00:01:28.741 --> 00:01:30.906
A and of B from shared memory

41
00:01:30.906 --> 00:01:33.720
so more warps also read more in total.

42
00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.468
On the largest shape

43
00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:38.063
eight warps read least as four by two

44
00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.326
thirty-two thousand seven hundred

45
00:01:40.392 --> 00:01:41.590
sixty-eight loads.

46
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.371
KAMI makes choices like

47
00:01:43.433 --> 00:01:44.740
this at compile time.

48
00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.280
The task removes KAMI's kernel.

49
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.156
Every output is checked

50
00:01:50.223 --> 00:01:51.616
against a CPU result

51
00:01:51.616 --> 00:01:54.470
and the final run adds four hidden shapes.

52
00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.711
The candidate and the reference

53
00:01:56.772 --> 00:01:59.272
run six times each, in alternating order

54
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:00.979
because a launch's position

55
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:02.260
can change its time.

56
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:03.332
In our toy

57
00:02:03.397 --> 00:02:06.087
balanced orders recover the true speed-up

58
00:02:06.153 --> 00:02:07.793
of one point two exactly

59
00:02:07.859 --> 00:02:10.024
while a lopsided two-to-one order

60
00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.730
gives one point one five.

61
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.056
A pass needs all eight shapes

62
00:02:14.124 --> 00:02:15.760
above a noise threshold.

63
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.820
Seven of sixteen models passed.

64
00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.920
Claude Opus 5.5 and Gemini 4 Argon

65
00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:25.141
running in Pi, both scored twenty-three

66
00:02:25.141 --> 00:02:27.970
against eighteen for the reference.

67
00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:30.800
Opus built its kernel around memory.

68
00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.558
It streams A and B into shared memory

69
00:02:33.623 --> 00:02:35.250
with asynchronous copies

70
00:02:35.315 --> 00:02:37.137
and loads the old C straight

71
00:02:37.202 --> 00:02:39.870
into its registers, so the reads overlap.

72
00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.994
Its own idea was to hand out

73
00:02:42.058 --> 00:02:43.452
the slices in reverse

74
00:02:43.515 --> 00:02:45.099
so the first blocks start

75
00:02:45.163 --> 00:02:46.620
on data still in cache.

76
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.370
But a good score is not a pass.

77
00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.309
Muse Wonder 1.0 scored twenty-one

78
00:02:52.387 --> 00:02:54.192
point seven and failed

79
00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.153
while GPT-6.1 Sol scored

80
00:02:56.231 --> 00:02:57.800
the same and passed.

81
00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.930
So: small double-precision products

82
00:03:01.003 --> 00:03:02.460
are bound by memory.

83
00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:04.684
The grid of warps decides the

84
00:03:04.749 --> 00:03:06.170
shared-memory traffic.

85
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.677
And a balanced launch order cancels

86
00:03:08.739 --> 00:03:10.280
a steady position effect.

87
00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.605
As an exercise

88
00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.750
compute the intensity of a block

89
00:03:13.815 --> 00:03:15.830
of two hundred fifty-six cubed.

90
00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.038
Next, sparse matrix multiplication

91
00:03:19.038 --> 00:03:20.100
in clusters.
